A||||6||Klown||225/50 or 205/55 tires?||||5||
Z||000000||Klown||03-16-2001||12:51 AM||||I'm getting ready to purchase a set of four 16 X 7 inch wheels and I was wondering what recommendations you guys could make about tire sizes.  I chose these two tire sizes (225/50-16 & 205/55-16) because they would both give me a tire diameter close to the original 215/60-15.  It's tempting to use the wider 225/50 tire but I was wondering if using the 205/55 tire would actually be better for performance.  Is the 225 to wide for the 7 inch wide wheel or maybe 205 is to narrow?  Will one be more responsive than the other?||||reg||5
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||03-16-2001||02:10 AM||||I have 225/50 16 x 7 on my Fiero and they give the car a good looking "footprint" for the lack of a better term. It just looks nice and agressive. Performance wise Im not sure how much 20 mm will effect performance when comparing 225 and 205. I would just go on the looks of a 225. It really compliments 16 in wheels. <P>Oddly enough I found that not all 225 are the same...My BFGoodrich VR4 Comp TA never rubbed in the front in the time that I drove them...when I had to replace them I wanted a little more treadwear and went with a no brand aggressive looking tire and it rubs just a little bit now when I turn the wheel to the extreme. Doesn't bother anything but I thought it was an interesting finding.<P>Hope that helps.||||reg||6
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||03-16-2001||09:06 AM||||I have 225/50/16 on the rear and 205/50/16 front, for easier steering and a slightly different appearance. The 225/50/16 do look nice!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buzzsig_1.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000003||Wade87GT||03-16-2001||09:08 AM||||I had 225/50-16 all around on 16x7 wheels for about 8 years. The handling was very neutral with an increased grip over the stock setup. I also have larger swaybars. I had considered using 205/55-16s in the front because I was a bit concerned about snap oversteer at the limit. Every 205/55 I've looked at was slightly taller then the 225/16. Imagine how much shorter the rears would look after they have worn down.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mr.Frosty||03-16-2001||02:26 PM||||I have a set of 16x7 OZ rims with 205\55 F<BR>and 225\50 rear.I also think that the fronts <BR>look taller too.I am going to get 225\50 <BR>for the front for uniformity.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||14
Z||000005||rogergarrison||03-17-2001||11:37 AM||||i have 205/60 front-225/60 rear. I wanted a fairly nice ride and to fill wheelwells in my kit Ferrari. The basic rules are lower the sidewall profile,less flex=better handling. But the taller the sidewall the softer the ride. And there are pretty big differences it tire brands of the same size as much as an inch in height. Another thing to look at is wider tires give less traction in snow and rain than thinner, easier to hydroplane as there is more area in contact with pavement although tread pattern does help somewhat. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||03-18-2001||01:53 AM||||My 16X7 rims have 225/50 in the rear,and 205/55 in the front,no problems,handles very nuetral,and the speedo is right on.||||reg||1
